      "created_at": "2016-10-17T03:56:01.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Having completed my \"Multiverse Saving Contest\" I found it interesting to play a specific series of games to evaluate the effectiveness of the various teams of heroes. &nbsp;I felt that experiment helped me determine which team is the most effective (at least the way I play them). So I figured why not run a similar experiment for the villains?</p><p>Here are the rules:</p><p>1. Video game decks only.</p><p>2. Other than the villain matches will be randomly generated.</p><p>3. Each villain will play 6 games: 1 regular and 1 advanced of: 3 Hero, 4 Hero, 5 Hero combinations.</p><p>4. The outcome of each game, heroes, and environments will be tracked.</p><p>5. Once I go through the 6 games against each villain once, I will decide if I want to repeat this process to increase the sample size.</p>\n\n\n<br>\n\n\t<table><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://greaterthangames.com/forum/topic/multiverse-saving-contest-a-battle-of-the-teams-9360\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"><span>Multiverse Saving Contest - A Battle of the Teams</span>&nbsp;</a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p><br>",
      "post_number": 1,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:48:16.741Z",
